{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming 4c37fe8d. Re-derived on current main 7e7b8412 before promising.\n\nThe defect is real and I found the exact sites. Resolution.Lookup(actor string, at int64) at host/identity/resolve.go:211 judges through effective at :247, which compares whole Unix seconds: \"if at \u003c anchor.from\" at :251 admits an anchor whose second equals the record's, and \"anchor.revoked != 0 \u0026\u0026 at \u003e= anchor.revoked\" at :257 withdraws an endorsement whose revocation shares the record's second. Anchor force and revocation are stored as int64 seconds (:58-60, :141, :192-193). A gitseq repository can write many records in one second, so a same-second anchor or revocation reaches backward and changes the authority of an act that had already happened when it was written.\n\nOne thing I want to state plainly, because it changes how I will approach this rather than just what I will change. The current behaviour is not an oversight. The comment above effective says so directly: \"Revocation wins a tie with the instant asked about ... Signed timestamps have one-second resolution, so ties are real, and the safe reading of a withdrawal is the one that takes effect.\" That is a deliberate conservative choice made because order inside a second was not knowable from a timestamp. The request is not to fix a bug in that reasoning; it is to supply the information the reasoning lacked. Once verified log position is available, ties are not real and the safe reading is not needed, because the true order is known. I will rewrite that comment to give the new reason rather than leave prose arguing for behaviour I removed.\n\nWhat I will build: a public position-aware lookup an outside application can call for a specific host.Record. Anchor force begins at its own verified log position, revocation ends it at its own position, and neither reaches backward across an equal-timestamp earlier record. Delegations and parent-chain withdrawal obey the same ordered boundary -- the existing parent recursion from :262 has to inherit the position rule, not just the timestamp one, or an agent credential outlives an ordered withdrawal of the anchor that minted it. Signed timestamp is retained only for NotAfter expiry, which is a real wall-clock claim rather than an ordering question. Deterministic offline replay and existing bounds preserved.\n\nTests: real-host regressions where a move or act precedes a same-second anchor, and where an act precedes a same-second revocation; hand-built equal-timestamp cases for the delegation and parent-withdrawal paths; and mutation proofs. I will break each ordered boundary deliberately and confirm the new assertions fail. These are exactly the tests most likely to pass by accident: written from a timestamp, every one of them is already green against the wrong behaviour.\n\nThis changes the public host/identity contract, so docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in the same head with its candidate artifact published, plus exact-head artifacts for host/identity. Gates: full and focused Go tests with -race, vet, gofmt, docs, git diff --check. Then independent review with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.\n\nNoted and respected: this blocks chess item 4's anchored-seat authority, which codex has now promised, so I will treat it as on that critical path. No chess semantics go into the host.\n\nBranch request/identity-position-aware-resolution in its own worktree, as the request records.","body":{"area":"host/identity","base":"7e7b8412bcd95e3d3345a481b72febc502513109","branch":"request/identity-position-aware-resolution","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d6504b10999b16540c8f190370807b4c37fe8d6e"}}